	<Fragment slot="hero-title">BUILDING AN<br />INDEPENDENT<br />ARTIST WORLD</Fragment>
	<div class="article-era">
		<p>Lately I’ve been thinking a lot about what actually makes an artistic project feel complete.</p>
		<p>Not successful, not big, not perfectly designed.</p>
		<p>Complete.</p>
		<p>I keep coming back to that idea because, while working on Fhugawz, I’ve realized how easy it is to treat every part of a project as a separate problem.</p>
		<p>You finish a song. Then you need a cover.</p>
		<p>You make the cover. Then you need something to post.</p>
		<p>Then comes a video, a caption, another song, another image, another release.</p>
		<p>And without really noticing it, you can end up with a huge amount of material without knowing what actually holds any of it together.</p>
		<p>That is the part I’ve become more interested in.</p>
		<p>Not how to make more things, but how to make the things I’m already making feel like they belong to the same place.</p>

		<h2>The song still comes first</h2>
		<p>For me, none of this works if the music does not work.</p>
		<p>A beautiful visual identity cannot save a song I do not believe in. A concept cannot replace a melody, a lyric or a production decision that genuinely makes me feel something.</p>
		<p>The song is still the center.</p>
		<p>But I no longer think the song has to exist alone.</p>
		<p>A lot of the music I remember most strongly is connected in my head to something else: a photograph, a color, a period of my life, the way an album looked, a strange video I once saw, a typeface. Even the feeling I had when I discovered that music for the first time.</p>
		<p>All of those things eventually attach themselves to the music.</p>
		<p>So when I think about Fhugawz now, I am not only thinking about individual songs. I am also thinking about the space that exists around them.</p>
		<p>What kind of world does this song belong to?</p>
		<p>What happened before it?</p>
		<p>What could happen after it?</p>
		<p>What should someone feel before they even press play?</p>
		<p>I do not think every artist needs to ask those questions. But for the kind of project I want to build, they are becoming increasingly important.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/building-an-independent-artist-world/building-independent-artist-world-visual-identity.webp" alt="Visual identity process for an independent music project." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>I don’t really see it as branding</h2>
		<p>Talking about an “artist world” can easily sound like another way of talking about branding.</p>
		<p>I understand why.</p>
		<p>There are logos, colors, photography, typography and all the things we normally associate with visual identity.</p>
		<p>But that is not really the part that interests me most.</p>
		<p>I am more interested in the consistency of a feeling.</p>
		<p>Two images can look completely different and still belong to the same project. Two songs can use very different instruments and still feel connected.</p>
		<p>The connection does not have to be obvious either.</p>
		<p>Sometimes it is a certain loneliness, a particular way light appears, a texture in the production, a question that keeps returning.</p>
		<p>Something difficult to explain, but easy to recognize after it has appeared enough times.</p>
		<p>That is much closer to what I mean when I talk about building a world.</p>

		<h2>Sound gives me the first clues</h2>
		<p>I usually begin to understand a project through sound.</p>
		<p>And not necessarily through genre.</p>
		<p>Genre can change too easily.</p>
		<p>What interests me more is the emotional character underneath it.</p>
		<p>How close should the voice feel? How much space should exist around the instruments? Should something feel fragile or aggressive? Should the production feel clean, damaged, distant or physical?</p>
		<p>Those kinds of decisions tell me much more than simply calling something rock, pop, shoegaze or electronic.</p>
		<p>Fhugawz moves through different sounds because the story itself also moves through different emotional states.</p>
		<p>And I like that.</p>
		<p>I do not want every album to sound like a variation of the previous one just to keep the project recognizable. I would rather find the things underneath genre that remain connected.</p>
		<p>That has been one of the most interesting parts of developing the project: discovering how far the sound can move before it stops feeling like part of the same universe.</p>
		<p>I still do not know exactly where that limit is.</p>
		<p>And I think that is part of the experiment too.</p>

		<h2>Images help me understand the music</h2>
		<p>Sometimes I do not fully understand a song until I start working on its images.</p>
		<p>That happens to me more often than I expected.</p>
		<p>A photograph can reveal something about a song that I was already feeling but had not yet managed to put into words.</p>
		<p>The same thing happens with a cover.</p>
		<p>When the right image appears, the music suddenly becomes more specific.</p>
		<p>You understand where it lives.</p>
		<p>That is why I am increasingly interested in treating visual direction as part of the creative process rather than something that simply happens after the music is finished.</p>
		<p>For me, the goal is not for every image to match.</p>
		<p>It is for them to feel related.</p>
		<p>They can change because the story changes. The palette, photography and typography can change; even the visual quality itself can transform radically from one album to another.</p>
		<p>But there should still be some invisible thread running through everything.</p>
		<p>I think that thread matters much more than strict consistency.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/building-an-independent-artist-world/building-independent-artist-world-content-system.webp" alt="Content system and creative direction for an artist brand." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>A story does not have to become lore</h2>
		<p>This is something else I have been thinking about a lot.</p>
		<p>When someone hears “narrative project,” it is easy to imagine that everything needs characters, timelines and twenty pages explaining the mythology.</p>
		<p>I do not think it has to work that way.</p>
		<p>Sometimes the story is simply emotional.</p>
		<p>A person begins somewhere and ends somewhere else. Something they believed in stops working. A memory changes meaning. A relationship breaks. An identity starts becoming unstable.</p>
		<p>That is already a story.</p>
		<p>With Fhugawz I am taking that idea further because the albums are connected, but I still want the music to work without anyone needing to read a manual before listening.</p>
		<p>The deeper narrative can be there for whoever wants to look for it.</p>
		<p>But someone else should also be able to enter through a song, an image or a moment and feel something without knowing the entire structure.</p>
		<p>That balance matters to me.</p>
		<p>I never want the concept to become homework.</p>

		<h2>I’m also changing how I think about content</h2>
		<p>This website is part of that change.</p>
		<p>For a long time I thought about content mainly as something created around a release.</p>
		<p>Now I am more interested in using it as a record of the project while it is actually happening.</p>
		<p>That is one of the reasons this Journal exists.</p>
		<p>I can write about a decision while I am still making it, show an experiment that might eventually fail, document how one version of a song turns into another or explain why I changed a visual direction that originally seemed to work.</p>
		<p>That feels much more interesting to me than pretending every idea arrived fully formed.</p>
		<p>But there is also something here that I feel conflicted about.</p>
		<p>At some point, being a musician started to come with the expectation that you also had to become a content creator.</p>
		<p>And I understand why.</p>
		<p>The way we discover music has changed enormously. A lot of discovery now happens inside platforms constantly competing for a few seconds of attention. If you make music and want someone to reach it, it is difficult to completely ignore those spaces.</p>
		<p>But I also do not want to confuse making music with feeding a content machine.</p>
		<p>I do not want to feel that a song I spent weeks or months working on needs to become twenty fifteen-second videos in order to justify its existence.</p>
		<p>Or that every creative decision has to be recorded, explained and immediately turned into something consumable.</p>
		<p>There is something strange about that.</p>
		<p>You can get thousands of views on a video and still build no real relationship between that person and your music.</p>
		<p>Someone can hear three seconds, keep scrolling and never think about what they just heard again.</p>
		<p>And I suppose that is one of the things I have been thinking about most lately.</p>
		<p>I do not want to design the project only for those three seconds.</p>
		<p>That does not mean ignoring social media or pretending content strategies are useless. They obviously can be useful, and I will probably use many of them.</p>
		<p>But I want them to be a door.</p>
		<p>Not the project.</p>
		<p>I still believe there are people who enjoy discovering music slowly: finding an artist, looking at a cover, listening to an entire album, reading something about what is behind it, coming back a few days later and discovering something else.</p>
		<p>Maybe those people are less visible inside all the noise now, but they are still there.</p>
		<p>I am one of them too.</p>
		<p>And if I genuinely believe in what I am building, I feel I need to leave room for that kind of relationship.</p>
		<p>Because after spending so much time on a song, an album or an entire universe, reducing it exclusively to something designed to stop a scroll for a few seconds feels strange.</p>
		<p>I would rather think long term.</p>
		<p>Maybe someone finds a song today and does not yet understand everything around it.</p>
		<p>Maybe they return months later.</p>
		<p>Maybe then they find another album, read one of these notes or discover that something that originally seemed isolated was actually part of a much larger story.</p>
		<p>For me, that is where a much more interesting connection begins.</p>
		<p>Not in desperately trying to capture attention for three seconds, but in building something solid enough that, when someone decides to look a little closer, there is actually something behind it.</p>
		<p>That is also the purpose of this Journal.</p>
		<p>Not to document absolutely everything.</p>
		<p>To document what feels worth remembering.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/building-an-independent-artist-world/building-independent-artist-world-release-strategy.webp" alt="Release strategy and project planning for an independent artist." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>I don’t think the world needs to be finished before showing it</h2>
		<p>This is probably the biggest change in the way I think about all of this.</p>
		<p>I used to see coherence as something that had to be completely designed from the beginning.</p>
		<p>Now I think an important part of it is discovered through repetition.</p>
		<p>You make something. Then you make something else.</p>
		<p>You begin to notice what still feels right and what starts feeling out of place.</p>
		<p>You keep some things, abandon others and slowly patterns begin to appear.</p>
		<p>For me, the important thing is paying attention to those patterns instead of trying to force every idea into the project simply because I like it individually.</p>
		<p>Not every good idea belongs to the same world.</p>
		<p>That has been a useful lesson.</p>

		<h2>What I’m trying to build with Fhugawz</h2>
		<p>At this point I see Fhugawz less as a collection of albums and more as one long work that keeps changing language.</p>
		<p>The music is one part, the visual direction is another and what I write on this website is part of it too. The characters, spaces, memories and ideas that keep returning are another part.</p>
		<p>I am still figuring out exactly how everything connects.</p>
		<p>And I honestly prefer it that way.</p>
		<p>I do not want to pretend I already know what the finished version of the project looks like.</p>
		<p>Part of the reason I want to document this process is so that I can look back later and discover how much of it was planned and how much simply appeared while I was working.</p>
		<p>For now, I keep returning to a fairly simple idea:</p>
		<p>The music should be strong enough to exist on its own.</p>
		<p>But everything around it can give it somewhere to live.</p>
		<p>And if that place eventually becomes specific enough, maybe people will not only remember the song.</p>
		<p>Maybe they will also remember where they were when they entered the world around it.</p>
	</div>
